{{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Prefecture'''|徳山県|Tokuyama Ken}} is a coastal [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the southern shore of [[Harujima]] Island, immediately adjacent to the [[Okami]] metropolitan region. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 1,192,484. It is the 28th largest prefecture by land area with an area of 2,421.49 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Tokuyama, Tokuyama|Tokuyama]]. Tokuyama Prefecture is renowned as Okaiken's premier center for plastics and precision model manufacturing, celebrated for its abundant {{nihongo|'''mackerel'''|鯖|saba}} fisheries, and its role as the nation's first railway terminus. The prefecture is often referred to as the {{nihongo|"Mould of the Sea"|海の鋳型|Umi no Igata}} for its dual identity as both a maritime powerhouse and an industrial forge.

==History==
[[File:Tokuyama Bay Historical.png|thumb|left|250px|Tokuyama Bay in the 16th century, showing the bustling port and early fishing settlements along the coast.]]

===The Maritime Gateway Period===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]], the coastal region now known as Tokuyama was governed by the {{nihongo|Tokuyama Clan|徳山氏|Tokuyama-shi}}, a formidable maritime lord lineage that controlled the vital sea lanes connecting the capital of [[Okami]] to the southern Harujima trade routes. Unlike the inland domains that relied on agricultural taxation, the Tokuyama lords built their wealth on the abundant mackerel runs that seasonal currents drove into the prefecture's deep-water bays, as well as on tolls extracted from merchant vessels seeking shelter in the region's natural harbors.

The clan's stronghold, {{nihongo|Tokuyama Castle|徳山城|Tokuyama-jō}}, was constructed in 1387 on a rocky promontory overlooking the bay. The fortress commanded a panoramic view of the shipping channels, allowing the Tokuyama lords to monitor and regulate all maritime traffic approaching the capital from the south. During the [[Sengoku period]], the Tokuyama Clan maintained a precarious neutrality by offering safe harbor to all belligerent fleets—a pragmatic strategy that preserved the region's commercial infrastructure while the rest of Harujima burned. The castle's famous {{nihongo|"Wave Gate"|波門|Nami-no-mon}}, a massive sea-level entrance accessible only by boat at high tide, became a symbol of the domain's intimate relationship with the ocean.

===The First Railway and Industrial Birth===
[[File:Tokuyama First Railway 1876.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The inaugural run of the Okami-Tokuyama Railway in 1876, the first rail line in Okaiken.]]

The transformation of Tokuyama from a feudal fishing domain into an industrial powerhouse began with the [[Saito Restoration]]. In 1876, the newly established Imperial government completed the nation's first railway line, connecting [[Okami]] to [[Tokuyama]]—a distance of approximately 64 kilometers. This engineering marvel, driven by the Saito government's urgent need to project central authority beyond the capital, fundamentally altered the prefecture's economic trajectory. The railway transformed Tokuyama from a remote coastal outpost into the capital's primary southern gateway, enabling the rapid transport of fresh seafood to Okami's burgeoning markets and importing industrial raw materials to the prefecture's new manufacturing zones.

The arrival of the railway catalyzed the development of the {{nihongo|Tokuyama Industrial Belt|徳山工業地帯|Tokuyama Kōgyō Chitai}}, a string of factories and shipyards that stretched along the coast from Tokuyama city to [[Kudamatsu]]. The Saito government established model factories for shipbuilding and cotton spinning in the prefecture, which were subsequently sold to the emerging zaibatsu conglomerates. By 1900, the Tokuyama belt had become one of the most productive manufacturing corridors in Harujima, producing textiles, processed seafood, and industrial chemicals for the domestic market.

===The Plastics Revolution===
[[File:Aoshima Factory Floor.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Aoshima model factory floor in Kudamatsu, 1962, during the height of the plastics manufacturing boom.]]

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] era brought a profound economic pivot to Tokuyama Prefecture. As the nation rebuilt its shattered industrial base, the prefecture's existing chemical and petrochemical infrastructure—originally developed for wartime production—was redirected toward peacetime applications. The breakthrough came in 1953, when researchers at the Tokuyama Chemical Institute developed a high-impact polystyrene formulation ideally suited for precision injection molding. This innovation triggered the {{nihongo|"Plastics Revolution"|プラスチック革命|Purasuchikku Kakumei}}, as dozens of small manufacturers established operations along the coast to exploit the new material.

The most significant of these was {{nihongo|'''Aoshima'''|青島}}, founded in 1958 in the city of [[Kudamatsu]]. Originally a small workshop producing plastic combs and household goods, Aoshima pivoted to scale model kits in 1961 after its founder recognized the growing domestic demand for detailed replica vehicles and military models. The company's commitment to engineering precision and historically accurate tooling set a new standard for the industry, and by the 1970s, Aoshima had become one of Okaiken's most recognized model manufacturers, exporting kits to markets across the Shendan Ocean. The success of Aoshima spawned an entire ecosystem of supporting industries—tool and die makers, specialty paint producers, and packaging facilities—transforming the Tokuyama coast into Okaiken's undisputed center for plastics and precision model manufacturing.

===Modern Development===
The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] through Tokuyama in 1967 cemented the prefecture's dual identity as both an industrial forge and a commuter haven for the capital. The Tokuyama Station became a critical junction on the high-speed rail network, enabling thousands of workers to commute daily to Okami while supporting the rapid shipment of manufactured goods to metropolitan markets. The establishment of the {{nihongo|Tokuyama Electric Railway|徳山電気鉄道|Tokuyama Denki Tetsudō}}, commonly known as {{nihongo|'''Tokutetsu'''|徳電|Tokuden}}, provided vital through-service connections to the Okami Metro network, further integrating the prefecture into the capital's transportation grid.

Today, Tokuyama Prefecture balances its industrial heritage with its maritime traditions, maintaining the largest mackerel landing port in Harujima while continuing to innovate in precision plastics manufacturing and advanced materials research.

==Geography==
[[File:Tokuyama Coastline.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The rugged coastline near Kaminoseki, showing the deep-water bays that have sustained the prefecture's fishing industry for centuries.]]

Tokuyama Prefecture occupies a coastal region on the southern shore of Harujima Island, featuring a diverse landscape of coastal plains, river deltas, and forested hills that gradually rise toward the interior. The prefecture's coastline extends for approximately 210 kilometers along the Shendan Ocean, characterized by numerous deep-water bays, rocky headlands, and sheltered harbors that have supported the region's maritime economy since antiquity.

The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Plain'''|徳山平野|Tokuyama Heiya}} forms the prefecture's economic and demographic heartland, a broad coastal lowland watered by the {{nihongo|Oze River|大瀬川|Ōze-gawa}} and its tributaries. The plain's fertile alluvial soils and flat terrain made it ideal for early settlement and industrial development, and it remains the most densely populated area of the prefecture. To the east, the terrain rises into the {{nihongo|Yanai Hills|柳井丘陵|Yanai Kyūryō}}, a range of low, forested hills that separate the Tokuyama Plain from the neighboring prefectures.

The prefecture includes several offshore islands, the most significant being the {{nihongo|Kaminoseki Archipelago|上関諸島|Kaminoseki-shotō}}, a chain of small islands off the southwestern coast. These islands maintain traditional fishing communities and are connected to the mainland by regular ferry services. The archipelago's tidal currents create some of the richest fishing grounds in Harujima, particularly for the mackerel runs that have defined the prefecture's culinary and economic identity.

