==Economy==
[[File:Tokuyama Mackerel Market.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Tokuyama Central Fish Market during the morning mackerel auction.]]

Tokuyama Prefecture possesses a diversified economy centered on maritime industries, precision plastics manufacturing, and its role as a commuter hub for the Okami metropolitan region. The prefecture's coastal geography has shaped its economic identity since antiquity, while its proximity to the capital has driven modern industrial development and residential expansion.

===Fishing and Seafood===
[[File:Tokuyama Mackerel Catch.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A traditional mackerel fishing vessel unloading the day's catch at Kaminoseki harbor.]]

The maritime sector remains the cultural and economic foundation of Tokuyama Prefecture. The deep-water bays and sheltered harbors along the prefecture's coastline support Okaiken's largest mackerel fishing fleet, with over 1,200 registered vessels operating from ports in [[Tokuyama]], [[Kudamatsu]], [[Kaminoseki]], and [[Yanai]].

The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Mackerel'''|徳山鯖|Tokuyama Saba}} is the prefecture's signature product, prized across Okaiken for its rich oil content and firm texture. The seasonal mackerel runs, driven by cooling ocean temperatures from May through October, bring massive schools into the prefecture's bays—a natural phenomenon that has sustained the region's economy for centuries. The mackerel is processed into a variety of products including fresh fillets for the metropolitan market, {{nihongo|saba-zushi|鯖寿司}} (vinegared mackerel sushi), {{nihongo|saba-miso|鯖味噌}} (mackerel simmered in miso), and dried {{nihongo|saba-himono|鯖干物}} (dried mackerel) that commands premium prices in specialty markets.

Beyond mackerel, the prefecture's fishing industry harvests significant quantities of sea bream, horse mackerel, and various shellfish from the Kaminoseki Archipelago's rich tidal grounds. The {{nihongo|Tokuyama Central Fish Market|徳山中央魚市場|Tokuyama Chūō Gyoshijō}}, located on the main harbor, handles approximately 180,000 tonnes of seafood annually, making it one of the largest fish markets in Harujima.

===Plastics and Model Manufacturing===
[[File:Aoshima Model Display.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A display of Aoshima precision model kits at the annual Tokuyama Hobby Expo.]]

The plastics and precision model manufacturing sector represents Tokuyama Prefecture's most distinctive industrial contribution to Okaiken. The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Plastics Corridor'''|徳山プラスチック回廊|Tokuyama Purasuchikku Kairō}}, a 30-kilometer stretch of manufacturing facilities extending from [[Kudamatsu]] through [[Hikari]] to [[Tokuyama]], is the nation's densest concentration of injection molding, tooling, and precision plastics companies.

The corridor's anchor is {{nihongo|'''Aoshima'''|青島}}, headquartered in Kudamatsu. Founded in 1958, Aoshima has grown from a small household goods manufacturer into Okaiken's premier producer of scale model kits, specializing in highly detailed vehicles, ships, and architectural models. The company operates a 45,000-square-meter production facility with over 200 injection molding machines, producing millions of model kits annually for both the domestic market and export across the Shendan Ocean. Aoshima's commitment to historically accurate tooling and engineering precision has earned it a devoted following among hobbyists worldwide.

The Aoshima ecosystem supports a network of specialized suppliers: {{nihongo|'''Kudamatsu Tooling'''|下松工具|Kudamatsu Kōgu}} produces precision steel molds for the model industry; {{nihongo|'''Hikari Polymers'''|光高分子|Hikari Kōbunshi}} formulates specialty polystyrene and ABS compounds optimized for fine detail reproduction; and numerous smaller firms produce decals, photo-etched parts, and acrylic paints. The prefecture hosts the annual {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Hobby Expo'''|徳山ホビー博|Tokuyama Hobī-Haku}}, the largest model and hobby exhibition in Okaiken, attracting over 120,000 visitors and showcasing the region's manufacturing prowess.

===Commuter Economy===
The prefecture's proximity to [[Okami]] has driven significant residential development, particularly along the rail corridors served by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] and the {{nihongo|Tokuyama Electric Railway|徳山電気鉄道|Tokuyama Denki Tetsudō}} (Tokutetsu). An estimated 185,000 Tokuyama residents commute daily to the capital for work, making the prefecture one of the largest bedroom communities in the Tokami region. This commuter economy has spurred the growth of commercial districts around major transit stations, as well as a robust service sector catering to the daily flow of workers.
Tokuyama's coastal fishing fleet and industrial plastics corridor have converged into a national center for biomimetic materials science. Headquartered in the port district, Tokuyama Marine Biomaterials operates large-scale chitin purification facilities that process tens of thousands of tons of discarded mackerel and crustacean carapaces from the prefecture's morning seafood auctions. By reacting deacetylated chitosan with colloidal nanosilica recovered from regional geothermal power plants, the prefecture produces high-tensile, marine-degradable biopolymers. These nanocomposites exhibit vibrant structural coloration through nanophotonic refraction rather than synthetic petrochemical dyes, supplying lightweight, scratch-resistant interior paneling to automotive manufacturers in Yonezawa and consumer electronics firms in Okami.

==Transportation==
[[File:Tokuyama Station Shinkansen.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Harujima Shinkansen Nozomi service departing Tokuyama Station.]]

Tokuyama Prefecture maintains extensive transportation connections to the Okami metropolitan region and the broader Harujima network, reflecting its dual role as an industrial center and commuter hub.

===Rail===
The prefecture is served by the '''Harujima Shinkansen''', with [[Tokuyama Station]] serving as a major stop for both Nozomi and Nanpu services. The Shinkansen provides rapid access to Okami (approximately 25 minutes) and to the southern economic centers of [[Anjo-Saikawa]] and [[Toyozawa]]. Tokuyama Station is one of the busiest on the Harujima line outside the capital, handling approximately 78,000 passengers daily.

The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Electric Railway'''|徳山電気鉄道|Tokuyama Denki Tetsudō}}, commonly known as {{nihongo|'''Tokutetsu'''|徳電|Tokuden}}, operates an extensive commuter rail network providing through-service connections to the Okami Metro system. This integration allows Tokuyama residents to travel seamlessly into the capital without transferring, making the Tokutetsu a vital artery for the prefecture's commuter economy. The network's rolling stock is maintained at the historic {{nihongo|Kudamatsu Depot|下松車両区|Kudamatsu Sharyō-ku}}, one of the oldest rail maintenance facilities in Okaiken.

Conventional Okaiken Railways (OR) services connect the prefecture's smaller cities, including [[Niihama]], [[Yanai]], and [[Iwakuni]], to the regional rail network.

===Road===
The '''Tokuyama Expressway''' runs along the coast, connecting the prefecture to the Okami metropolitan expressway network to the north and to the [[Okazaki]] region to the east. The highway is a critical freight route for both the fishing industry's refrigerated transport and the plastics manufacturing sector's distribution network. The '''Kaminoseki Coastal Road''' provides scenic access to the archipelago's ferry terminals and fishing communities.

